HostFS - using folders from this computer
=========================================

HostFS lets you use folders on the computer running your web browser
as if they were discs in RISC OS. A mounted folder appears on the icon
bar and in the Filer as HostFS::<name>.$ and you can use it like the
hard disc: open files in applications, save into it, copy, move,
rename and delete files in the Filer, and run BASIC and Obey
applications that are kept there.

Changes you make in RISC OS are written back to the real folder, so
the files are there for your other programs too.

Contents:

  1) Mounting a folder
  2) Using a mounted folder
  3) File types and file names
  4) Commands
  5) Limits and troubleshooting


1) Mounting a folder
--------------------

There are three ways to mount a folder. Which ones you can use depends
on your web browser.

  a) Pick a folder (Chrome, Edge and other Chromium browsers)

     Click SELECT on the HostFS icon on the icon bar (the computer next
     to the RAM disc). Your browser asks you to choose a folder, and
     then asks whether this page may change the files in it. Say yes.

     The folder gets an icon of its own on the icon bar, labelled with
     its name, and a directory display opens.

     The browser remembers the folder. When you come back later, it
     is mounted again straight away or, if the browser wants to ask
     again, its icon shows an empty drive: click SELECT on it and
     allow access.

     Some folders can't be picked: the browser won't let you choose
     the whole of your home folder, Desktop or Documents, or system
     folders. Choose a folder inside them instead.

  b) Folders given to the server (any browser)

     If you start the desktop with the server supplied with it, you can
     name folders when you start it:

       node serve.mjs --host Work=~/riscos-files
       node serve.mjs --host Work=~/riscos-files --host-ro Photos=~/Pictures

     --host mounts a folder that RISC OS can change; --host-ro mounts
     it read-only. These folders are mounted each time the desktop
     starts. To mount or dismount them yourself, click MENU on the
     HostFS icon and use the Server folders submenu, or use *HostMount
     (see section 4).

     This works in every browser and also keeps the dates of the files
     you save. The server only answers requests from your own computer,
     and only for the folders you named.

  c) A read-only copy (Firefox and Safari)

     Firefox and Safari don't let a web page change files on your
     computer. Instead, choose "Mount read-only..." from the HostFS
     icon's menu (or click SELECT on the HostFS icon), or drag a folder
     from your computer onto the desktop. The folder's files as they
     are at that moment can be read, but not changed. Choose the folder
     again to see later changes.

     In Chrome and Edge, a folder dragged onto the desktop is mounted
     read-only to start with, because the browser only asks for
     permission to change files after a mouse click. Click MENU on the
     folder's icon and choose "Allow changes".

If a folder holds more than 20,000 files and directories, you are asked
before it is mounted, as reading a folder that size can take a while
and use a lot of memory. Click Continue to mount it anyway.


2) Using a mounted folder
-------------------------

Click SELECT on a folder's icon on the icon bar to open it. Click MENU
on the icon for:

  Open $          open the folder's root directory
  Rescan          look again for changes made on this computer
  Free            show how much space the disc it is on has free
  Dismount        stop using the folder (the files stay where they
                  are). For a folder waiting for permission this is
                  "Forget", which stops the browser remembering it.
  Allow changes   (dropped folders only) ask for permission to change
                  the files

You can drag files onto a folder's icon to copy them into it, just as
with the hard disc (hold Shift to move them instead).

If you change the folder with other programs while it is mounted,
RISC OS sees the changes when you open a directory display of the
directory concerned, when you come back to the browser window, or
straight away where the browser (or the server) can watch the folder.
Rescan on the icon's menu checks the whole folder.

Files that your computer has marked as read-only are shown as locked.

Items on the Pinboard that belong to a mounted folder are kept while
the folder is not mounted, and come back when it is mounted again.


3) File types and file names
----------------------------

Your computer keeps file types in file names rather than with the file.
HostFS uses the same rules as the HostFS of the RPCEmu and VirtualRPC
emulators, so folders can be shared with them.

  A name ending in a comma and three hex digits gives the file type.
  The ending isn't shown in RISC OS:

    Letter,fff      is the Text file  Letter
    Sprites,ff9     is the Sprite file  Sprites
    Game,ffb        is the BASIC file  Game

  A name ending in a comma and two hex numbers is a file without a
  file type (the numbers are its load and execution addresses):

    Code,00008000-00008000

  Otherwise the type comes from the file's extension, the part after
  the last dot. For example:

    .txt  Text (&FFF)        .html  HTML (&FAF)      .csv  CSV (&DFE)
    .png  PNG (&B60)         .jpg   JPEG (&C85)      .gif  GIF (&695)
    .pdf  PDF (&ADF)         .zip   Zip (&A91)       .json JSON (&F75)
    .svg  SVG (&AAD)         .mp3   AMPEG (&1AD)     .wav  WaveForm (&FB1)

  Any other file is a Text file.

When you save a file from RISC OS, it is given a ",xxx" ending only
when its type can't be told from its name. A PNG called photo/png is
saved as "photo.png", a Text file called Notes as "Notes", but a Sprite
file called Sprites is saved as "Sprites,ff9". Changing a file's type
with the Filer's "Set type" renames the file on your computer to
match.

Some characters are swapped between the two kinds of name:

  On your computer    In RISC OS
  ----------------    ----------
        .                 /       (photo.png is photo/png)
      space           hard space
        #                 ?
        $                 <
        ^                 >

Other characters RISC OS doesn't allow in names (such as & : * % @ and
letters outside the Latin-1 alphabet) are shown as _ in RISC OS. The
file keeps its real name on your computer. Files your computer uses
for its own purposes, such as .DS_Store, are not shown.

If two files would have the same name in RISC OS (for example "Notes"
and "Notes,fff"), only the first one found is shown.


4) Commands
-----------

These can be typed at the command line (press F12) or in a task window.

  *HostFS
      Selects HostFS: the current directory becomes the root of the
      first mounted folder.

  *HostMount [<name>]
      With no name, asks you to choose a folder (as clicking the HostFS
      icon does). With a name, mounts the server folder of that name
      (see 1b above).

  *HostDismount <name>
  *Dismount HostFS::<name>
      Dismount a folder.

  *HostMounts
      Lists the mounted folders.

Mounted folders can be used with all the usual commands, for example:

  *Cat HostFS::Work.$
  *Copy ADFS::HardDisc4.$.ReadMe HostFS::Work.$.ReadMe
  *Dir HostFS::Work.$.Letters


5) Limits and troubleshooting
-----------------------------

 - With a folder chosen in the browser (1a), files you save get
   today's date: the browser can't set the date of a file. Renaming a
   directory copies it and then deletes the old one, so it can be slow
   for a large directory. Folders given to the server (1b) have
   neither limit.

 - Programs written in ARM code (Absolute, Module and Utility files)
   can't be run from HostFS, just as they can't be run from the hard
   disc. BASIC and Obey programs work.

 - If the browser is refused permission, or the folder is moved or
   renamed on your computer while it is mounted, you'll see an error.
   Dismount the folder and mount it again.

 - If the server is stopped, folders given to it can't be read or
   changed until it is started again. Changes you made meanwhile are
   tried again for a short time.

 - Everything in a mounted folder is listed when it is mounted, and
   files are read the first time they're used, so very large folders
   take longer to mount and use more memory. Mount the folder you need
   rather than a whole disc.
